 <P>	Lab Proposal<P>Introduction<BR>	Deforestation has been a hot topic =
in the scientific community in last fifty years.  Much research has been=
 made in the area dealing with the affects of deforestation on the envir=
onment and biodiversity.  The water-cycle is an essential resource to al=
l life including humans.  If this cycle is disrupted, a ripple effect ca=
using cataclysmic effects to the environment can be seen through species=
 extinction, low biodiversity, and on a large scale global warming, whic=
h affects the whole biosphere.  <BR>This project's purpose or problem as=
ks: Does deforestation affect the rate of evaporation in a given area?  =
This led to our research question, in which we ask:  What (if any) are t=
he affects of deforestation on the evaporation rate in a heavily foreste=
d area compared to a developed area?  The hypothesis that was proposed f=
or this topic was that if water is placed in a deforested area, then the=
re will be a change in evaporation rate compared to that of a wooded are=
a.  The prediction made for this experiment was that when water is place=
d in a deforested area the evaporation rate will be greater than that of=
 the water placed in a heavily forested area.<BR>The Earth's water cycle=
 is an essential process that provides a means of life to creatures ever=
ywhere.  Also, rainforests contribute to biologically important gasses i=
n the Earth's atmosphere: They produce a large percentage of the availab=
le O2 on the planet via photosynthesis.  "When the forests are destroyed=
, the rate at which Carbon dioxide (CO2) can be drawn from the atmospher=
e is diminished. CO2 is a &quot;greenhouse&quot; gas that helps regulate=
 the temperature of the Earth's atmosphere. This gas is a byproduct of a=
ll respiring organisms, as well as a byproduct of the combustion of orga=
nic matter, such as fossil fuels. If CO2 builds up in the atmosphere, th=
e average temperature on the surface of the Earth can increase, affectin=
g most aspects of human life (Rubin, 2004)."  <BR>This is interesting be=
cause it holds relevance in the changing world today.  Deforestation is =
a growing threat to the Earth's biosphere, especially in the tropical ec=
osystems.  Deforestation not only transforms the geography of the enviro=
nment but also affects the climate.  Any slight imbalance to the Earth's=
 natural cycles could end in catastrophe to the global environment.   In=
formation gathered from this experiment can help determine just how detr=
imental an effect deforestation can have on the Earth's water cycle.  <B=
R>Purpose/Problem- Does deforestation affect the rate of evaporation in =
a given area?<BR>Hypothesis- If water is placed in a deforested area, th=
en there will be a change in evaporation rate compared to that of a wood=
ed area.<BR>Research Question- What are the affects of deforestation on =
evaporation rate in heavily forested and developed areas?<BR>Predictions=
- When water is placed in a deforested area the evaporation rate will be=
 greater than that of the water placed in a heavily forested area.<BR>Wh=
y is this interesting to you? - This is interesting because it holds rel=
evance in the changing world today.  Deforestation is a growing threat t=
o the Earth's biosphere, especially in the tropical ecosystems.  Informa=
tion gathered from this experiment can help determine just how detriment=
al deforestation can have on the Earth's water cycle.  The Earth's water=
 cycle is an essential process that provides a means of life to creature=
s everywhere.  Also, Rain forests contribute to biologically-important g=
asses in the Earth's atmosphere: they produce a large percentage of the =
available O2 on the planet via photosynthesis<P><P> <P>Background Inform=
ation:<BR>	With the study of water supplies and its effects on deforesta=
tion, there are many background factors for ideas to consider. Important=
 information for this lab includes forests, deforestation, the water cyc=
le, past experiments, and why our lab fits into a more universal applica=
tion. <BR>	Forests, (including tropical seasonal forests, temperate/hard=
wood forests, and moist forests) are important biomes that contain parti=
cular organisms, landscapes, and weather. The Columbia Electronic Encycl=
opedia defines forests as a dense growth of trees, together with other p=
lants, covering a large part of land, forming a canopy.  A forest is an =
ecosystem, providing shelter, food, and warmth. Though, the measured siz=
e of a forest varies from person to person and from institution to insti=
tution (Yahner 2). Our focus is on American temperate forests. Types of =
trees found in a temperate forest include elm, oak, basswood, maple, ash=
, beech, and coniferous trees (Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia). Approx=
imately forty percent of the eastern half of the United States is forest=
land, bordered by grasslands and prairies (Yahner). In the United States=
, forest coverage has dwindled from roughly half of the land to about a =
third today, compared to three hundred years ago (Columbia Electronic En=
cyclopedia). American temperate forests, and other forests in general, a=
re crucial because, "The chief economic product of forests is timber, bu=
t the economic benefits in terms of climate control, pollution abatement=
, and wildlife maintenance" for the region- and more importantly, the Ea=
rth- are affected (Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia). The benefits of fo=
rests can be spread into other numerous areas, including the fact that t=
hey act as watersheds. Forests act as watersheds by forming a humus laye=
r with loose soil, and have water-retaining roots that preserve adequate=
 water supplies. "The presence of a forest could smooth out the peaks in=
 runoff, thereby reducing floods and extending the period of runoff when=
 there are droughts", which has been experimented with (Mencunni pg 59).=
 Forests are also important because they have irreplaceable supplies of =
oxygen and soil nutrients. <BR>	With forests being such a vital form of =
supplies, especially timber, there is economic interest in using the pro=
vided materials and land. Through this, forests have been stripped, maki=
ng them responsible for quick re-growth or depletion. The process of rem=
oving trees for agriculture and construction that are not replaced is ri=
ghtly called deforestation. Deforestation has increased rapidly in the l=
ast fifty years. In fact, roughly one-fifth of the world's forests remai=
n (Kohm, Kathryn). Our current forests in the United States were logged =
in the nineteenth century, causing a lack of old and stable trees in the=
 area (Kohm, Kathryn). It is important to have old and stable trees beca=
use newly deforested areas are not as capable of retaining water as in t=
he past. <BR>	Water and the water cycle is one of the most crucial reaso=
ns for why Earth can sustain life. There are four main parts to the cycl=
e: precipitation, infiltration, condensation, and evaporation. Evaporati=
on is the process of surface water becoming vapor. Approximately 750 mm =
of water fall on the earth each year, and two-thirds of that returns to =
the atmosphere as evaporation, the other portion being absorbed by the s=
oil for vegetation (Wallace). Without vegetation, areas can't retain the=
 water and therefore experience run-off, drought, and flooding. <BR>	The=
 third background area is more of a wider glimpse of why the study of de=
forestation and its effects on the water cycle are so important. Forests=
 are one way of affectively dealing with global warming. Global warming =
has occurred because of the burning of fuels and the deforestation of fo=
rests, the effects of which have caused an alteration in the composition=
 of the earth's atmosphere, particularly troposphere. Therefore, the car=
bon cycle has changed noticeably with an increase in carbon dioxide, wat=
er vapor, and ozone (Yahner).  With this, there has been an increase in =
surface temperatures, leading to an increase in the evaporation of water=
, which already accounts for eighty percent of the earth's heat energy (=
http://www.questia.com/carboncycle/).<BR>	Experimentation with evaporati=
on is a rather new concept. Only in the past fifty years has the study o=
f evaporation unraveled (Mencuccini). In the 1950's, Jack Rutter, a plan=
t physiologist, found that on rainless days the transpiration was simila=
r to open water evaporation, whereas on rainy days the sum of the transp=
iration and evaporation of intercepted rainfall exceeded the normal amou=
nt (Mencuccini). Therefore, the amount of water processed increases with=
 the amount of water available. Through this, areas that lack retention =
sources, such as trees, experience rapid evaporation of water. In 1962, =
the Wallingford Centre for Ecology and Hydrology compared evaporation ra=
tes between forests and grasslands. They hypothesized that the potential=
 evaporation rates would differ depending on the length of tree roots, w=
hich were considered the controlling factor on the retention of the mois=
ture (Mencuccini). More recently on Western Campus, there was a student-=
generated lab on the location and rates of evaporation. Studying the eva=
poration and its effects is quite difficult because of there is such a v=
ariety of factors and angles as to where to focus. By looking at data fr=
om past experiments, disturbances vary greatly depending on "type, inten=
sity, spatial pattern, and the legacy or past organisms and structures t=
hat are left behind" (pg 8, Kohm, Kathryn). Therefore, it is very import=
ant to remain simple to avoid error, varying on spatial extent and tempo=
ral duration. <BR>Overall, through our lab we hope to gain a better unde=
rstanding of why our local environments are so important to understandin=
g global implications. Issues with evaporation rates in one area of the =
world are more likely than not to affect another thousands of miles away=
. Hence, deforestation is a popular topic that has implications that are=
 visible and testable today. Deforestation of tropical regions significa=
ntly affects precipitation at mid- and high latitudes. Specifically it h=
as been tested that the deforestation "of Amazonia and Central Africa se=
verely reduces rainfall in the lower U.S. Midwest during the spring and =
summer seasons and in the upper U.S. Midwest during the winter and sprin=
g, respectively, when water is crucial for agricultural productivity in =
these regions" (Avissar and Werth, 2004).  In Amazonia, the wet season, =
which is also their summer, had a decrease of precipitation levels as hi=
gh as 50% to 60% in the months of December and January.  Because of this=
 deforestation in the Amazon regions, the northern regions in America fe=
el the most losses in precipitation.  Amazonia is just one of many areas=
 being deforested every day and the effects of this precipitation loss i=
mpacts people all over the world.  Past research suggests that deforesta=
tion would lead to a decrease of about 600 mm of rainfall per year along=
 with a small increase in temperature.  Without a significant amount of =
rainfall, agriculture productivity declines because of the lack of water=
 to grow crops.  One can also see the effects of deforestation on dam wa=
ter.  Certain dams lost a significant percentage of water due to defores=
tation in the past few years.  Water in the dams could not be retained f=
or long because of the lack of water supply from the "catchment" areas t=
hat have been deforested.  "Forests act like sponges.  Rainwater is trap=
ped in the soil and released regularly" (Kasmo, 2003).  When forests are=
 being cut down for agriculture, timber industries, and urbanization pur=
poses, the forest floor loses its ability to absorb water and therefore =
more of it is evaporated.  This is important because during dry season, =
people need water supplies to support their societies.  It has been prov=
en that there is a positive correlation between the size of forests and =
the amount of water in dams.  Finally, climactic change from deforestati=
on can have an indirect effect on water supplies.  The warmer the climat=
e is the higher the increase in drought severity and frequency.  Even th=
e smallest percentage in evaporation change would affect large "fluxes" =
of water vapor.  The amount of leaf area is essential for absorption in =
forested areas.  Because many of these forested areas are being used for=
 agriculture purposes, the compacted ground cannot absorb water as easil=
y as it would in dense leaf areas.  Evapotranspiration occurs when plant=
s secrete or "transpire" water through pores in their leaves.  The water=
 draws heat as it evaporates cooling the air in the process.  "Since eva=
potranspiration is proportional to leaf area, the water recycled through=
 forest is much greater than that recycled through pasture, especially i=
n the dry season when pasture is dry while forest remains evergreen.  Th=
is is aggravated by the much higher runoff under pasture" (Fearnside, 19=
95). The compacted ground forces water to run-off therefore making it un=
available for later release into the atmosphere.  Because there is no wa=
ter to aid in the cooling process through evapotranspiration, the climat=
e becomes warmer and evaporation occurs much more quickly.  This ultimat=
ely results in the dry season to become larger and more severe.    <P>Bo=
oks:<P>Kohm, Kathryn. Creating a Forestry for the 21st Century. Washingt=
on, D.C: Island 	Press, 1997.<BR>	This book is an excellent resource for=
 the history of forests and their role in 	industry. It does so by expla=
ining the balance between logging and re-growth. <P>Markham, Adam. Poten=
tial Impacts of Climate Change on Tropical Forest Ecosystems.  	The Neth=
erlands: AA Dordrech, 1998.<BR>	This book focuses on Latin and South Ame=
rican ecosystems and the race against 	deforestation to sustain the envi=
ronment. <P>Mencuccini, Moncrief, and McNughton. Forest at the Land-Atmo=
sphere Interfere.<BR>	Cambridge, MA: CABI Publishing, 2004.<BR>	This boo=
k has a look at the carbon cycle and its implications at various levels.=
 It 	also has a chapter on water retention. <P>Moran and Ostrom. Seeing =
the Forest and the Trees: Human Enviroment Interactions in 	Forest Ecosy=
stems.Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 2005.<BR>	This book is a general guideli=
ne on how forests should be cared for in really hard 	language. <P>Peter=
ken, George F. Woodland Conservation and Management. London; New York: 	=
Chapman & Hall, 1993.<BR>	This book has a lot of graphs on different lev=
els of deforestation and how to 	recover. It is an economic book in most=
 senses, without much environmental 	implications. <P>Soussan, J.  and B=
.K. Shrestha, L.P. The Social Dynamics of Deforestation: A Case 	Study f=
rom Nepal. New York: Parthenon Pub. Group, 1995. <BR>	This book is an in=
depth account on how a relatively poor country uses their 	resources wit=
hout planning and the aftermath from this. It was helpful to see 	severa=
l stages of progress and regress along with how international groups are=
 the 	real forces behind studying deforestation. <P>Tucker, Richard P. a=
nd J.F. Richards. Global Deforestation and the Nineteenth-Century 	World=
. Durham, NC: Duke Press, 1983.<BR>	This book is a historical look at wh=
at colonial development has done to 	landscapes. A lot of land was clear=
ed for agriculture that was then abandoned. 	Now, there are a large perc=
entage of new forests that lack old trees and 	sustainability, much like=
 Ohio. <P>Tucker, Richard P. and J.F. Richard. World Deforestation in th=
e Twentieth Century?. 	Durham, NC: Duke University Press, 1988?.<BR>	Thi=
s book is a briefing of the previous installment with more data and futu=
re 	assessments. The focus is more on construction than agriculture. <P>=
Yahner, Richard H. Eastern Deciduous Forest: Ecology and Wildlife Conser=
vation. 	Minneapolis: University of Minnesota Press, 2000. <BR>	This boo=
k was helpful in explaining our experimental landscape's structure. It 	=
	focused on wildlife more than climate, making a small portion of the bo=
ok helpful. <P>Journals:<P>Avissar, Roni and David Werth, 2004,  Global =
Hydroclimatological Teleconnections 	Resulting from Tropical Deforestati=
on. Duke University.<BR>	This article is helpful in explaining the globa=
l effect on deforestation at one of its 	most potent regions: the rainfo=
rest. It also explains the implications of tropical 	deforestation on ot=
her latitudes that are far away yet connected. <P>Binns, J.A, P.M. Illin=
ger and E.L. Nel, 2001, Water Shortage, Deforestation and 	Development: =
	South Africa's Working For Water Programme, Rhodes 	University, Grahams=
town, South Africa.<BR>	This article is great for explaining what happen=
s to areas that are instable because 	they lack natural retention like f=
orests, for our background information and larger 	implications. It also=
 helps explain what we are finding through our test stations. <P>Cleugh,=
 Helen, Dale Hughes, Ray Leuning, and Steven J. Zegelin. 2005, Carbon an=
d Water 	fluxes Over a Temperate Eucalyptus Forest and a Tropical Wet/Dr=
y Savanna in 	Australia Measurements and Comparison with MODIS Remote Se=
nsing 	Estimates, vol. 129, issue3/4, pp 151-173. <BR>	This is another a=
rticle on the affects of evaporation globally. It  deals more with 	the =
carbon cycle that water cycle but has good information on heat and water=
 	vapor. <BR>	<BR>Delzon, Sylvain, and Denis Loustau, 2005, Age-Related =
Decline in Stand Water Use; Sap 	Flow and Transpiration in a Pine Forest=
 Chronosequence, vol. 129, issue 3-4, pp 	105-119.<BR>	This article reve=
als that older trees transpire less than younger trees as background 	in=
formation. <P>Fearnside, P.M. 1995, Potential Impacts of Climactic Chang=
e on Natural Forests-Forestry 	in Brazilian Amazonia.<BR>	This article h=
elps us form our hypothesis on which areas will evaporate more 	rapidly =
and with greater quantity because of past experiments. <P>Jarvis, P.G. a=
nd Teklehaimanot, 1991,  Direct Measurement of Evaporation of 	Intercept=
ed Water from Forest Canopies, vol. 28, issue 2, pp 603-618.<BR>	This ar=
ticle is helpful in explaining the location of evaporation within a smal=
l 	area, depending on whether it is the soil or a tree canopy. It also e=
xplains forest 	densities. <P>Kasmo, Mohd Arip, 2003, Development Proces=
s Analysis of Water Shortages and Their 	Impact on Future Water Supplies=
, vol. 14, issue 4, pp 477-487. <BR>	This article explains that deforest=
ation in the tropics is reflecting on water 	supplies in other parts of =
the world. It fits into the larger picture of our project. <P>Sun.G, S.G=
. McNulty, J. Lu, D.M. Amatya, Y. Liang, R.K. Kolka, 2005, Regional 	Ann=
ual Water Yield from Forest Lands and its Response to Potential 	Defores=
tation 	across the Southeastern United States, vol. 308, issue 1-4, pp25=
8-	268.<BR>	This journal explains water yield, precipitation input and e=
vapotranspiration 	output, in scale. It explains the regions that have t=
he highest yields, which are 	mountainous. The authors found out the hig=
hest yields by creating a model for 	spatial and temporal variability. I=
t is very helpful in explaining the importance of 	a contained area and =
climate change. <P>Wallace, J.S. 1995, Calculating Evaporation: Resistan=
ce to Factors, vol. 73, issue 3-4, pp 	353-356.<BR>	This article is help=
ful in deciphering a good experimental design when working 	with evapora=
tion. It mentions past experiments and their failures and gives good 	fo=
rmulas for calculating evaporation differently. <P>Zhang, X.C. and W.Z. =
Liu, 2005, Simulating Potential Response of Hydrology, Soil 	Erosion, an=
d Crop Productivity to Climate change in Changwu Tableland Region 	on th=
e Loess Plateau of China, vol. 131, issue 131, pp 127-142.<BR>	The clima=
te of a given area affects the conservation of an area. The article has =
	three variables; hydrology, soil loss, and crop production. Through it,=
 it was 	learned the importance of tillage for run offs and erosion leve=
ls. This article was 	another deciding factor in where to place our test=
 stations. <BR>Rubin, K. (2004). Importance of rainforests. Retrieved Oc=
t. 06, 2005, from Ask-An-EarthScientist Web site: http://www.soest.hawai=
i.edu/GG/ASK/askanerd.html.<P>Rubin, Dr. Ken. &quot;Importance of rainfo=
rests.&quot; Ask-An-Earth Scientist. 21 October 2004. University of Hawa=
ii, Department of Geology and Geophysics. 06 Oct. 2005 http://www.soest.=
hawaii.edu/GG/ASK/askanerd.html<P><P><BR>Websites:<P>Deforestaion. 1 Feb=
ruary 2001. 5 October 2005. EnviroFacts. http://www.botany.uwc.ac.za/Env=
facts/facts/deforestation.htm.<BR>	This website has basic definitions fo=
r deforestation and its effects. It is helpful in 	understanding the num=
erous ways in which deforestation can be dealt with, 	including climate =
control. <P><BR>Deforestation. 5 October 2005. Wikipedia. http://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Deforestation.<BR>	This website gives a basic definition =
of deforestation but does so very effectively 	by giving regional exampl=
es and applications. <P>Deforestation. 5 October 2005. Photovalet. http:=
//www.photovault.com/Link/Technology/Outside/Deforestation/TODVolume01.h=
tm.<BR>	This website is interesting simply because it is a collection of=
 before and after 	photographs and so on, that express the effects of de=
forestation. It is more 	interesting than applicable. <BR>Deforestation.=
 20 October 2005. JRScience. http://www.users.muohio.edu/rattercd/labpac=
ket.html. <BR>	This website explains a very similar past experiment that=
 occurred on Western 	<BR>	Campus and gave us inspiration on what things=
 we should and shouldn't do. <P>Deforesting the Riverscape: the Effects =
of Wood on Fish Diversity in a Venezuelan 	Piedmont Stream. 23 February =
2004. 5 October 2005. Science Direct. 	http://www.eeb.cornell.edu/flecke=
r/pdf/Wright&Flecker2004BioCons.pdf#search	=3D'deforestationexperiment'.=
<BR>	This website has an article about the study of fish and the lack of=
 branches falling 	in a river because of deforestation and the effects o=
f this. It is good background 	information for broader effects of defore=
station. It has a section of usable 	formulas. <P>Water Cycle. 28 August=
 2005. 5 October 2005. http://ga.water.usgs.gov/edu/watercycle.html.<BR>=
	This website is an elementary level educational tool about the water cy=
cle that 	helped us remember the steps we have forgotten when explaining=
 our project. <P><BR>Materials and Methods<BR>Our project will involve a=
 series of water testing stations located in varying degrees of developm=
ent.  We have placed five 4.5x 4.5x2 inch plastic containers in each of =
9 different locations on Western Campus.  The three categories are fores=
ted, semi-developed, and developed.  The three-forested areas are:  The =
brush by Kumler Chapel, the brush by Ernst Nature Amphitheater, and in t=
he brush above Ernst Nature Amphitheater.  The semi-developed areas are:=
  The quad between Peabody and Boyd Halls, behind the bell tower in fron=
t of Peabody Hall, and the hillside near the art museum (just after you =
cross the bridge).  The developed areas are:  The parking lot at Mary Ly=
on Hall, the drive that passes by Boyd Hall on the south, and the parkin=
g lot between Peabody and Boyd Halls.  Each container is filled with 150=
 mL of distilled water, and weighed. They were placed in their respectiv=
e areas at 7:30 am, on Monday, October 4.  Each member of the group is r=
esponsible for checking the status of the containers, at 5:30 pm, from M=
onday through Thursday.  On Thursday, we will collect the containers, an=
d wash them, refill them, and they will be returned the following Monday=
 morning.  When each member of the group is checking the testing sites, =
they are responsible for weighing each container, to the nearest one hun=
dredth of a gram.  There are data sheets made to fill in which include t=
he date, the overall weather conditions, the testing site, the container=
 code, and the current weight.  That will be compared to the weight of t=
he previous day, and the difference will then be recorded.  We will be d=
oing this for 16 days, which will be over a four-week period.  Of course=
, there are uncontrollable factors, such as the weather, for which will =
have to account for.  If it rains, we will empty the containers, refill =
the containers, and not include the data for the day the rain fell.  Lea=
ves and large debris will be removed from each container, but smaller pi=
eces of grass, dirt, or leaf matter will be left in the container.  Also=
, if some data appears to be skewed, or if we know that something has ha=
ppened to one of the containers (i.e. one has tipped over, or something =
was added), that container will not be counted.  When we have finished c=
ollecting data, we will calculate the means of the five containers in ea=
ch site for each day.   Various t-tests, ANOVA tests, and other statisti=
cal analyses will be used to compare the means of each set of data, and =
then graphed to make easily readable charts.<BR>	Our class-teaching day =
will involve a "field trip" of sorts, including our classmates in the te=
sting process.  It will be a simple procedure where each of the students=
 will have their chance to go hiking around to each of our testing spots=
, and will be responsible for recording the data at that time.  Unfortun=
ately, the class time is too early to use their findings, but it will be=
 a learning experience for all.  During this time, we would also like to=
 have some discussion over what their assumptions are about deforestatio=
n, and the effects on evaporation, then share some of the information we=
 have gathered.  <BR>    <BR>Research Design:<P>In order to measure the =
effects of deforestation on water supplies, we chose to use Miami Univer=
sity's campus to test how different developed areas affect water evapora=
tion.  To conduct this experiment, we decided to use plastic containers =
that held measured amounts of distilled water in them.  By placing these=
 containers outside for controlled time periods of four days per week, w=
e would measure there before and after weight to distinguish how much of=
 that water had evaporated while the containers were outside.  We are co=
nducting this experiment to test if different areas, forested and develo=
ped, have different impacts on the rate of water evaporation due to incr=
eased or decreased amounts of shade provided by the trees. Three main ca=
tegories were used to measure the rate of water evaporation.  The first =
category involves areas that had been altered due to construction.  Thes=
e areas were labeled "developed" and therefore will be exposed to more s=
unlight and heat without any tree coverage.  We designated three separat=
e areas as developed. These areas included: the parking lot outside of B=
oyd Hall, a sidewalk next to Boyd Hall, and another parking lot in front=
 of Mary Lion Hall. Within each area we used five separate containers of=
 water in order to test how quickly water would evaporate.  We would lin=
e the containers in a row with some space in between each container as t=
o avoid any condensation from one container affecting the amount of wate=
r in the container next to it.  In total we used fifteen containers of w=
ater to measure the rate of water evaporation in the designated develope=
d areas.  We also placed containers in areas that we designated "semi-de=
veloped."  These areas included grassy parts with some tree coverage lik=
e: the grass quad next to Boyd Hall, the bell tower in front of Peabody =
Hall, and a grassy area next to the pond downhill from Peabody Hall.  Th=
ese areas would provide some shade to the containers during the day but =
were still not considered forested because the land, although including =
nature, is constantly altered by man.  Again we used five separate conta=
iners in each area resulting in a total of fifteen containers measuring =
the rate of evaporation in semi-developed areas.  Finally we put more co=
ntainers in designated "forested" areas in order to compare how constant=
ly shaded areas like these, where the sun cannot really penetrate throug=
h the trees, affects evaporation rates.  We used various forested areas =
around Peabody Hall and in each area placed five more containers.  With =
these last fifteen containers accounting for the forested water containe=
rs, we used a total of forty-fife containers filled with distilled water=
 in order to conduct this experiment.  Every Sunday night we will fill e=
ach of the containers with a measured amount of one hundred and fifty mL=
 of distilled water and then, in order to be as accurate as possible, we=
 would weigh the containers on an electric scale.  We then recorded thes=
e various weights and used them for our starting container weights.  Mon=
day morning we placed the containers in their designated areas and each =
day at the end of the day, we would measure them again on the electric s=
cale and figure out the difference in water weight.  The containers will=
 remain outside and be measured at the end of every day up until the las=
t day, Thursday night, when we will bring the containers back inside for=
 the weekend to prepare for the same set up Monday morning of the follow=
ing week.  This process will go on for four weeks, resulting in a total =
of 16 days of data collected data.  The factor that will alter this proc=
ess is rain.  In this case we would collect the containers for that day,=
 refill them with the one hundred and fifty mL of distilled water, and p=
ut them back out the next morning.  By collecting this data we will be a=
ble to compare how significantly deforestation affects water supplies no=
t just on the Miami Campus, but also in relation to the world. <P> =

